<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="arsenite methylation (bacterial)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Description>&lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; While the jury is still out about whether Napoleon Bonaparte died of arsenic poisoning or cancer |CITS: [367316][6750413]|, it is agreed by all that arsenic is a potent toxicant. Indeed, the results of exposure to high arsenic concentration in drinking water in West Bengal, India, and Bangladesh are considered by some to be the worst public health disaster in recent history |CITS: [15276412]|. The form of arsenic influences the type and severity of toxicity. Arsenate (As&lt;sup&gt;V&lt;/sup&gt;) is a structural analog of phosphate, and inhibits phosphorylation processes. For example, ADP-arsenate spontaneously hydrolyzes, resulting in uncoupling of oxidative phosphorylation |CITS: [518594]|, while arsenite (As&lt;sup&gt;III&lt;/sup&gt;) has a very high affinity for thiol groups, and thus binds to and inhibits many key enzymes that have thiol groups in their active sites. In addition, inorganic arsenic is a potent carcinogen |CITS: [12505313]|. Arsenic exists in two oxidation states, As&lt;sup&gt;III&lt;/sup&gt; and As&lt;sup&gt;V&lt;/sup&gt;. In aerobic environments most of the inorganic arsenic is found as As&lt;sup&gt;V&lt;/sup&gt;, and in neutral pH the common species is |FRAME: ARSENATE|. Most (but not all) of the inorganic As&lt;sup&gt;III&lt;/sup&gt; in neutral pH exists as the non-charged species |FRAME: CPD0-2040|. Both As&lt;sup&gt;V&lt;/sup&gt; and As&lt;sup&gt;III&lt;/sup&gt; compounds can enter the cell. |FRAME: ARSENATE Arsenate| is chemically similar to phosphate, and enters the cell via the phosphate transport systems, such as the |FRAME:TAX-562| |FRAME: PITA-MONOMER| and |FRAME: ABC-27-CPLX| systems. |FRAME: CPD0-2040 &quot;Arsenious acid&quot;| is a polyol similar to glycerol, and can enter cells via bidirectional channels that allow water and small uncharged molecules like glycerol to pass through (such as |FRAME: EG10396 GlpF| of |FRAME: TAX-562| and aquaglyceroporins). The absorption rate of |FRAME: ARSENATE| is much lower than that of |FRAME: CPD0-2040|, and the role of phosphate transporters in the uptake of arsenic appears to be limited. For example, bacterial periplasmic phosphate-binding proteins discriminate phosphate over |FRAME: CPD0-2040| more than 500- to 850-fold (up to 4500-fold in the extremely arsenic-resistance species |FRAME: TAX-1118153|) |CITS: [32435915]|. Both prokaryotes and eukaryotes reduce intracellular arsenate to arsenite. However, prokaryotes and single-cell eukaryotes can pump arsenite out of the cell by using a dedicated pump (such as the |FRAME: G3O-34321-MONOMER Acr3| or |FRAME: CPLX-7 ArsB|), multicellular organisms require other means of getting rid of the metal. Certain mammals, including humans, methylate arsenite to several methylated forms that are oxidized to non-toxic methylated As(V) forms and excreted from the organism in the urine. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; Inorganic arsenic enters the cell in the form of |FRAME: ARSENATE| and |FRAME: CPD0-2040|. Since there are no known export systems for |FRAME: ARSENATE|, cells reduce it to |FRAME: CPD0-2040|, which is either exported from the cell or methylated. The reduction of |FRAME: ARSENATE| to |FRAME: CPD0-2040| is described in several other pathways, classified under |FRAME: Arsenate-Detoxification|. Methylation of arsenic compounds has been documented in many organisms, including bacteria, fungi, green plants, and higher organisms (for a detailed list of organisms and the corresponding arsenic species, see |CITS: [Cullen89]|). The process is so common that in certain environments methylated arsenic species become the dominant form of arsenic |CITS: [Anderson91]|. The pathway shown here is limited to the methylation of inorganic arsenic. The key enzyme in the methylation pathway is |FRAME: EC-2.1.1.137|. The enzyme was initially identified in mammals |CITS: [11790780][15808521]|. |FRAME: GLUTATHIONE Glutathione|, the major thiol in most organisms, is present at millimolar concentrations and reacts readily with AS&lt;sup&gt;III&lt;/sup&gt; compounds to form glutathione complexes inside cells |CITS: [7841340]|. In organisms that do not produce glutathione AS&lt;sup&gt;III&lt;/sup&gt; compound believe to form complexes with other thiol compounds such as |FRAME: CoM|. The arsenic-thiol complex is believed to be the substrate for the methyltransferase, which binds As&lt;sup&gt;III&lt;/sup&gt; in a series of three thiol transfer reactions |CITS: [15526190]|. Once bound to the enzyme, As&lt;sup&gt;III&lt;/sup&gt; undergoes one to three methylation events. Most of the substrate remains bound to the enzyme after the first methylation, with only a small fraction being released as |FRAME: METHYLARSONITE|, and is released after the second methylation as |FRAME: DIMETHYLARSINOUS-ACID|. An even smaller fraction proceeds to a third methylation event and is released as |FRAME: CPD-22659|, which is a volatile compound. During the reaction the arsenic atom changes valencies between (III) and (V) several times, being oxidized during methylation and reduced back to (III) valency with electrons derived from thioredoxin. In the presence of oxygen the released products |FRAME: METHYLARSONITE| and |FRAME: DIMETHYLARSINOUS-ACID| can be oxidized nonenzymatically to |FRAME: METHYLARSONATE| and |FRAME: DIMETHYLARSINATE|, respectively |CITS: [25325836]|. Otherwise, these compounds can react again with glutathione, forming |FRAME: CPD-24323| and |FRAME: CPD-24324|, which can serve as an alternative substrates for the methyltransferase. The methylation process forms three products - |FRAME: METHYLARSONITE|, |FRAME: DIMETHYLARSINOUS-ACID|, and |FRAME: CPD-22659|. The latter is a volatile compound, and as such as less toxic. However, both the monomethylated and dimethylate forms of As&lt;sup&gt;III&lt;/sup&gt; are highly toxic. When exposed to oxygen, they are oxidized to the much less toxic pentavalent forms. However, the mechanisms for exporting the compounds out of the cell have not been studied yet, with the exception of |FRAME: G-80528 ArsP|, which can export |FRAME: METHYLARSONITE|. However, it appears that ArsP is used by bacteria to detoxify the compounds generated by other organisms and is thus not included in this pathway.</Description>
